// Class 11 Physics practice bank, part 2: chapters 8-14 (Mechanical Properties of Solids to Waves).
StudyData.addPractice(11, "physics", {
  "mechanical-properties-of-solids": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The SI unit of Young's modulus is:", options: ["N", "N m⁻²", "N m⁻¹", "it has no unit"], answer: 1, explain: "Y = stress/strain. Strain is dimensionless, so Y has the unit of stress, N m⁻² (pascal).", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following physical quantities has no unit?", options: ["Stress", "Young's modulus", "Strain", "Bulk modulus"], answer: 2, explain: "Strain is a ratio of two like quantities (change in dimension ÷ original dimension), so it is a pure number.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Steel is considered more elastic than rubber because:", options: ["it is heavier than rubber", "it can be stretched to a greater length", "it breaks at a smaller stress", "it shows a smaller strain for the same stress"], answer: 3, explain: "Elasticity is the ability to resist deformation and recover. Steel has a much larger Young's modulus, so the same stress produces a much smaller strain.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which modulus of elasticity is relevant for liquids and gases?", options: ["Bulk modulus", "Young's modulus", "Shear modulus", "Modulus of rigidity"], answer: 0, explain: "Fluids have no fixed shape, so they have no Young's or shear modulus. Only volume elasticity (bulk modulus) applies.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Poisson's ratio of a stretched wire is defined as:", options: ["longitudinal strain ÷ lateral strain", "lateral strain ÷ longitudinal strain", "stress ÷ strain", "change in volume ÷ original volume"], answer: 1, explain: "Poisson's ratio = lateral strain/longitudinal strain. It is a pure number.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A steel wire of length 2 m and radius 1 mm is stretched by a force of 100 N. If Y = 2 × 10¹¹ Pa, the extension is about:", options: ["0.32 mm", "0.64 mm", "3.2 mm", "0.16 mm"], answer: 0, explain: "ΔL = FL/(πr²Y) = (100 × 2)/(3.14 × 10⁻⁶ × 2 × 10¹¹) = 200/(6.28 × 10⁵) = 3.2 × 10⁻⁴ m ≈ 0.32 mm.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Two wires A and B of the same material are stretched by the same force. A has length L and radius r; B has length 2L and radius 2r. The ratio of extension of A to that of B is:", options: ["1 : 2", "1 : 1", "2 : 1", "4 : 1"], answer: 2, explain: "ΔL ∝ L/r² for the same F and Y. For A it is L/r²; for B it is 2L/4r² = L/2r². Ratio A : B = 2 : 1.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Steel is more elastic than rubber.\nReason (R): Rubber can be stretched to a much larger length than steel before breaking.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 1, explain: "A is true because steel shows a much smaller strain for a given stress (larger Y). R is also true, but a large stretch does not mean greater elasticity, so it does not explain A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A lift is supported by a steel cable of cross-section 2 cm² carrying a load of 2000 kg (g = 10 m s⁻²). The elastic limit of the steel is 3 × 10⁸ Pa. By what factor does the elastic limit exceed the stress in the cable?", options: ["6", "1.5", "0.33", "3"], answer: 3, explain: "Stress = mg/A = 2 × 10⁴ N / (2 × 10⁻⁴ m²) = 10⁸ Pa. Factor = 3 × 10⁸/10⁸ = 3.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A rod 1.0 m long and 2.0 cm in diameter is stretched by 1.0 mm and its diameter decreases by 6 μm. The Poisson's ratio of the material is:", options: ["0.003", "0.03", "0.3", "3"], answer: 2, explain: "Lateral strain = 6 × 10⁻⁶/0.02 = 3 × 10⁻⁴. Longitudinal strain = 1 × 10⁻³/1.0 = 10⁻³. Ratio = 0.3.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A wire of length 1.0 m and cross-section 1.0 mm² (Y = 2 × 10¹¹ Pa) is slowly loaded with 100 N. The elastic energy stored in it is:", options: ["5.0 × 10⁻² J", "2.5 × 10⁻² J", "2.5 × 10⁻⁴ J", "0.50 J"], answer: 1, explain: "ΔL = FL/(AY) = 100 × 1/(10⁻⁶ × 2 × 10¹¹) = 5 × 10⁻⁴ m. Energy = ½FΔL = ½ × 100 × 5 × 10⁻⁴ = 2.5 × 10⁻² J (not FΔL, since the force rises from 0 to F).", level: 3 },
      { q: "A cube of side 10 cm of a material with bulk modulus 1.6 × 10¹¹ Pa is placed in a liquid whose pressure is raised by 8 × 10⁷ Pa. The decrease in volume of the cube is:", options: ["5.0 cm³", "0.05 cm³", "0.20 cm³", "0.50 cm³"], answer: 3, explain: "V = 10⁻³ m³. ΔV = pV/B = 8 × 10⁷ × 10⁻³/1.6 × 10¹¹ = 5 × 10⁻⁷ m³ = 0.50 cm³.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A machine rests on a rubber pad of area 50 cm² and thickness 2 cm (shear modulus 2 × 10⁶ Pa). When vibrations apply a sideways force of 100 N, the top face of the pad is displaced sideways by:", options: ["0.2 mm", "2 mm", "0.02 mm", "1 mm"], answer: 0, explain: "Shear strain θ = F/(AG) = 100/(5 × 10⁻³ × 2 × 10⁶) = 0.01 rad. Displacement = θ × thickness = 0.01 × 2 cm = 0.02 cm = 0.2 mm.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Young's modulus of a wire does not change when its length is doubled.\nReason (R): Young's modulus depends on the nature of the material, not on its dimensions.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Y = (F/A)/(ΔL/L) is a property of the material. Changing length or area changes the extension but not Y, so R explains A.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define Young's modulus and state its SI unit.", a: "Young's modulus is the ratio of longitudinal stress to longitudinal strain within the elastic limit: Y = (F/A)/(ΔL/L) = FL/(AΔL). SI unit: pascal (N m⁻²).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State Hooke's law.", a: "Within the elastic limit (for small deformations), stress is directly proportional to strain; the constant of proportionality is the modulus of elasticity of the material.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between ductile and brittle materials, giving one example of each.", a: "Ductile material: a large gap between the ultimate strength and fracture points on the stress–strain curve, so it can be drawn into wires (copper, mild steel).\nBrittle material: ultimate strength and fracture points are very close, so it breaks soon after the elastic limit with little plastic deformation (glass, cast iron).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "The bulk modulus of water is 2.2 × 10⁹ Pa. Find the fractional decrease in its volume when the pressure on it is increased by 2.2 × 10⁷ Pa.", a: "B = p/(ΔV/V), so ΔV/V = p/B\n= 2.2 × 10⁷ / 2.2 × 10⁹ = 0.01.\nThe volume decreases by **1%**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A steel wire (Y = 2 × 10¹¹ Pa) of length 4 m and cross-section 2 mm² carries a load of 20 kg (g = 10 m s⁻²). Find the stress, strain and elongation.", a: "F = mg = 200 N; A = 2 × 10⁻⁶ m².\nStress = F/A = 200/(2 × 10⁻⁶) = **1 × 10⁸ Pa**.\nStrain = stress/Y = 10⁸/(2 × 10¹¹) = **5 × 10⁻⁴**.\nElongation ΔL = strain × L = 5 × 10⁻⁴ × 4 = 2 × 10⁻³ m = **2 mm**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Show that the elastic potential energy stored per unit volume of a stretched wire is ½ × stress × strain.", a: "1. The work done in stretching by x is W = ½Fx = ½ × (stress × A) × (strain × L), since the force rises uniformly from 0 to F.\n2. This work is stored as elastic potential energy: U = ½ × stress × strain × (AL).\n3. AL is the volume of the wire, so energy per unit volume = **½ × stress × strain** = ½ Y (strain)².", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the main regions of the stress–strain curve of a metal wire.", a: "1. Straight-line part up to the proportional limit: stress ∝ strain (Hooke's law).\n2. Up to the elastic limit (yield point) the wire regains its original length on unloading; beyond it the strain increases rapidly and the deformation is permanent (plastic).\n3. The maximum stress is the ultimate tensile strength; after this point the wire thins down and breaks at the fracture point.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Define Young's modulus, shear modulus and bulk modulus. State the type of strain involved in each and give the SI unit.", a: "1. **Young's modulus** Y = (F/A)/(ΔL/L): longitudinal stress ÷ longitudinal strain (change in length).\n2. **Shear modulus (rigidity)** G = (F/A)/θ: tangential stress ÷ shearing strain (angular deformation θ = Δx/L); the shape changes, the volume does not.\n3. **Bulk modulus** B = −p/(ΔV/V): hydraulic stress (pressure) ÷ volume strain; the volume changes, not the shape.\n4. All three have the unit of stress, pascal (N m⁻²), because strain is dimensionless.\n5. Only bulk modulus applies to liquids and gases; compressibility = 1/B.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "A beam of length l, breadth b and depth d, supported at its ends and loaded at the middle by W, sags by δ = Wl³/(4bd³Y). Using this, explain why bridge girders have an I-shaped cross-section and find how the sag changes if (a) the depth is doubled, (b) the breadth is doubled.", a: "1. For a given load and material, the sag is δ ∝ 1/(bd³): it falls very rapidly with the depth d.\n2. (a) Depth doubled: d³ becomes 8 times, so the sag becomes **1/8** of the original.\n3. (b) Breadth doubled: the sag becomes **1/2** of the original. So increasing the depth is far more effective than increasing the breadth.\n4. An I-shaped girder gives a large depth with less material and weight.\n5. The wide flanges at top and bottom resist bending and buckling, so a strong, economical, light bridge or building frame results.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A student studies a metal wire of length 2.0 m and cross-section 1.0 mm² and records the extension for different loads:\n20 N → 0.2 mm; 40 N → 0.4 mm; 60 N → 0.6 mm; 80 N → 1.6 mm.\n(i) Find the Young's modulus from the first reading.\n(ii) At which load does the wire cross its elastic limit? Give a reason.\n(iii) Find the stress in the wire at the 60 N load.", a: "(i) Y = FL/(AΔL) = (20 × 2.0)/(10⁻⁶ × 2 × 10⁻⁴) = 40/(2 × 10⁻¹⁰) = **2 × 10¹¹ Pa**.\n(ii) At **80 N**: the extensions were proportional to the load up to 60 N (0.2 mm per 20 N), but 80 N should give 0.8 mm and gives 1.6 mm, so Hooke's law no longer holds and the wire has gone past its elastic limit.\n(iii) Stress = F/A = 60/10⁻⁶ = **6 × 10⁷ Pa**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "mechanical-properties-of-fluids": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The SI unit of the coefficient of viscosity is:", options: ["N m⁻¹", "N s", "Pa s", "N m⁻² s⁻¹"], answer: 2, explain: "From F = ηA(dv/dx), η = F/(A·dv/dx) has unit N s m⁻² = Pa s (poiseuille).", level: 1 },
      { q: "A hydraulic lift works on the principle of:", options: ["Pascal's law", "Bernoulli's principle", "Stokes' law", "Torricelli's law"], answer: 0, explain: "Pressure applied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted undiminished everywhere (Pascal's law), so a small force on a small piston balances a large force on a large piston.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The equation of continuity, A₁v₁ = A₂v₂, is a statement of conservation of:", options: ["energy", "momentum", "mass", "charge"], answer: 2, explain: "For an incompressible fluid, the mass (volume) entering a pipe per second equals the mass leaving it.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The excess pressure inside a soap bubble of radius r and surface tension S is:", options: ["2S/r", "S/r", "8S/r", "4S/r"], answer: 3, explain: "A soap bubble has two free surfaces (inside and outside), so the excess pressure is 2 × (2S/r) = 4S/r.", level: 1 },
      { q: "With a rise in temperature, the coefficient of viscosity of:", options: ["a liquid increases and of a gas decreases", "a liquid decreases and of a gas increases", "both liquid and gas decreases", "both liquid and gas increases"], answer: 1, explain: "In liquids the cohesive forces weaken on heating, so η falls; in gases more molecular transfer between layers occurs on heating, so η rises.", level: 1 },

      { q: "In a hydraulic press the small and large pistons have areas 1 cm² and 50 cm². A force of 200 N on the small piston can support a load of:", options: ["2 × 10⁴ N", "1 × 10⁴ N", "4 N", "1 × 10⁶ N"], answer: 1, explain: "Pressure is transmitted equally: F₂ = F₁(A₂/A₁) = 200 × 50 = 10⁴ N.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Water leaks from a small hole in a tank, 5 m below the free surface of the water. Taking g = 10 m s⁻², the speed of efflux is:", options: ["5 m s⁻¹", "7.1 m s⁻¹", "10 m s⁻¹", "14 m s⁻¹"], answer: 2, explain: "Torricelli's law: v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 10 × 5) = √100 = 10 m s⁻¹.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A strong wind blowing over a roof can lift it off.\nReason (R): Where the speed of air is higher, its pressure is lower (Bernoulli's principle).",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Fast wind above the roof lowers the pressure there; the higher pressure of still air inside pushes the roof up. This is Bernoulli's principle, so R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A gardener's hose has an inner diameter of 2 cm and water flows through it at 1 m s⁻¹. She fits a nozzle of diameter 0.5 cm. The speed of water leaving the nozzle is:", options: ["4 m s⁻¹", "8 m s⁻¹", "64 m s⁻¹", "16 m s⁻¹"], answer: 3, explain: "A₁v₁ = A₂v₂ and A ∝ d², so v₂ = v₁(d₁/d₂)² = 1 × (2/0.5)² = 16 m s⁻¹.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Water (S = 0.07 N m⁻¹, contact angle 0°, density 1000 kg m⁻³) rises in a capillary tube of radius 0.5 mm. Taking g = 10 m s⁻², the height of rise is:", options: ["1.4 cm", "2.8 cm", "5.6 cm", "0.28 cm"], answer: 1, explain: "h = 2S cosθ/(ρgr) = 2 × 0.07 × 1/(1000 × 10 × 5 × 10⁻⁴) = 0.14/5 = 0.028 m = 2.8 cm.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A glass bead of radius 3 mm and density 2500 kg m⁻³ falls through a liquid of density 1000 kg m⁻³ and viscosity 1.5 Pa s. Taking g = 10 m s⁻², its terminal velocity is:", options: ["2 cm s⁻¹", "0.2 cm s⁻¹", "4 cm s⁻¹", "1 cm s⁻¹"], answer: 0, explain: "vₜ = 2r²(ρ − σ)g/9η = 2 × (3 × 10⁻³)² × 1500 × 10/(9 × 1.5) = 0.27/13.5 = 0.02 m s⁻¹ = 2 cm s⁻¹.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The surface tension of a soap solution is 0.03 N m⁻¹. The work done in blowing a soap bubble from radius 1 cm to radius 2 cm is about:", options: ["1.1 × 10⁻⁴ J", "5.7 × 10⁻⁵ J", "7.5 × 10⁻³ J", "2.3 × 10⁻⁴ J"], answer: 3, explain: "A bubble has two surfaces. Increase in area = 2 × 4π(R₂² − R₁²) = 8π(4 − 1) × 10⁻⁴ = 24π × 10⁻⁴ ≈ 7.5 × 10⁻³ m². Work = S × ΔA = 0.03 × 7.5 × 10⁻³ ≈ 2.3 × 10⁻⁴ J.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A venturi meter is a horizontal pipe whose wide section has area 40 cm² and narrow section has area 10 cm². Water (1000 kg m⁻³) flows at 2 m s⁻¹ in the wide section. The pressure difference between the two sections is:", options: ["1.5 × 10⁴ Pa", "3.0 × 10⁴ Pa", "6.0 × 10⁴ Pa", "3.2 × 10⁴ Pa"], answer: 1, explain: "Continuity: v₂ = 2 × 40/10 = 8 m s⁻¹. Bernoulli (same height): ΔP = ½ρ(v₂² − v₁²) = 500 × (64 − 4) = 3.0 × 10⁴ Pa.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The terminal velocity of a small sphere falling in a viscous fluid is proportional to the square of its radius.\nReason (R): Terminal velocity is independent of the viscosity of the fluid.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "vₜ = 2r²(ρ − σ)g/9η, so vₜ ∝ r² (A is true). But vₜ ∝ 1/η, so it does depend on viscosity (R is false).", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "State Pascal's law.", a: "The pressure applied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted undiminished to every point of the fluid and to the walls of the containing vessel.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is meant by terminal velocity?", a: "The constant maximum velocity attained by a body falling through a viscous fluid, when the viscous drag plus the upthrust (buoyancy) balances its weight so that the net force is zero.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Find the gauge pressure and the absolute pressure at a depth of 20 m in a lake. (Atmospheric pressure = 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa, ρ = 1000 kg m⁻³, g = 10 m s⁻².)", a: "Gauge pressure = ρgh = 1000 × 10 × 20 = **2 × 10⁵ Pa**.\nAbsolute pressure = P₀ + ρgh = 1 × 10⁵ + 2 × 10⁵ = **3 × 10⁵ Pa**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Mercury does not wet glass, but water does. Explain the difference in the behaviour of the two liquids in a narrow glass tube.", a: "For water, the angle of contact is less than 90°, so cohesion is weaker than adhesion; the meniscus is concave and the water rises in the tube (h = 2S cosθ/ρgr is positive).\nFor mercury, the angle of contact is greater than 90°, so cohesion dominates; the meniscus is convex, cosθ is negative and the mercury is depressed below the outside level.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Use Bernoulli's equation to derive Torricelli's law for the speed of efflux of a liquid from a small hole at a depth h below the free surface of a wide tank.", a: "1. Apply P + ½ρv² + ρgy = constant between the top surface (point 1) and the hole (point 2). Both are open to air, so P₁ = P₂ = P₀.\n2. The tank is wide, so the surface speed v₁ ≈ 0.\n3. P₀ + ρg h = P₀ + ½ρv² (taking the level of the hole as the reference), so v = **√(2gh)**, the speed gained by a body falling freely through height h.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "The two pistons of a hydraulic lift have radii 2 cm and 20 cm. A car of mass 1200 kg (g = 10 m s⁻²) is placed on the large piston. Find the force needed on the small piston, and how far the small piston moves for the car to rise by 1 cm.", a: "Area ratio A₂/A₁ = (20/2)² = 100.\nWeight of car F₂ = 1200 × 10 = 12 000 N.\nF₁ = F₂ × (A₁/A₂) = 12 000/100 = **120 N**.\nThe volume of liquid moved is equal on both sides: A₁d₁ = A₂d₂, so d₁ = 100 × 1 cm = **100 cm (1 m)**.\nThe small force acts over a larger distance (work is conserved).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Show that the excess pressure inside a liquid drop of radius r and surface tension S is 2S/r.", a: "1. Let the radius of the drop increase slowly from r to r + Δr under excess pressure P.\n2. Work done by the excess pressure = P × 4πr² × Δr (force × displacement of the surface).\n3. Increase in surface energy = S × [4π(r + Δr)² − 4πr²] ≈ S × 8πrΔr.\n4. Equating, P × 4πr²Δr = S × 8πrΔr, so **P = 2S/r**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Derive an expression for the height to which a liquid rises in a capillary tube of radius r, in terms of the surface tension S, angle of contact θ and density ρ.", a: "1. A liquid of density ρ rises to height h in a tube of radius r with a concave meniscus; angle of contact θ.\n2. Along the circumference of contact (length 2πr) the surface tension acts at angle θ to the wall; the upward component of force is S cosθ × 2πr.\n3. By Newton's third law this pulls the liquid column up with a force 2πrS cosθ.\n4. Weight of the column of liquid raised = πr²hρg.\n5. In equilibrium, 2πrS cosθ = πr²hρg, so **h = 2S cosθ/(ρgr)**. The rise is greater for a narrower tube. For mercury θ > 90°, cosθ is negative and h is negative, i.e. a depression.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Using Stokes' law, derive the expression for the terminal velocity of a sphere of radius r and density ρ falling through a fluid of density σ and viscosity η. Hence find the ratio of the terminal velocities of two identical raindrops of radii 1 mm and 2 mm.", a: "1. At terminal velocity the forces balance: weight = upthrust + viscous drag.\n2. (4/3)πr³ρg = (4/3)πr³σg + 6πηr vₜ.\n3. So 6πηr vₜ = (4/3)πr³(ρ − σ)g.\n4. **vₜ = 2r²(ρ − σ)g/(9η)**, so vₜ ∝ r².\n5. For radii 1 mm and 2 mm (same ρ, σ, η): vₜ₁ : vₜ₂ = r₁² : r₂² = 1 : 4. The larger drop falls 4 times faster.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Blood flows through an artery of radius 4 mm at a speed of 0.2 m s⁻¹. Because of a deposit, the artery narrows to a radius of 2 mm at one place. Take blood density = 1060 kg m⁻³ and treat the flow as steady and non-viscous with the artery horizontal.\n(i) Find the volume flow rate through the artery.\n(ii) Find the speed of blood in the narrow part.\n(iii) Find the fall in pressure in the narrow part.", a: "(i) Q = Av = π(4 × 10⁻³)² × 0.2 = π × 16 × 10⁻⁶ × 0.2 = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ m³ s⁻¹ (about 10 cm³ s⁻¹).\n(ii) A₁v₁ = A₂v₂, and A ∝ r², so v₂ = 0.2 × (4/2)² = **0.8 m s⁻¹**.\n(iii) Bernoulli: P₁ − P₂ = ½ρ(v₂² − v₁²) = ½ × 1060 × (0.64 − 0.04) = 530 × 0.6 = **318 Pa**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "thermal-properties-of-matter": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Water has its maximum density at:", options: ["0 °C", "4 °C", "100 °C", "−4 °C"], answer: 1, explain: "Water contracts on heating from 0 °C to 4 °C and expands above 4 °C, so its density is greatest at 4 °C.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The SI unit of specific heat capacity is:", options: ["J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹", "J K⁻¹", "J kg⁻¹", "W m⁻¹ K⁻¹"], answer: 0, explain: "s = ΔQ/(mΔT), so its unit is J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. J K⁻¹ is the unit of heat capacity and W m⁻¹ K⁻¹ that of thermal conductivity.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which mode of heat transfer does not require a material medium?", options: ["Conduction", "Convection", "Radiation", "Diffusion of air"], answer: 2, explain: "Radiation travels as electromagnetic waves, which is how heat reaches us from the Sun through space.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A temperature of 50 °C on the Fahrenheit scale is:", options: ["122 °F", "90 °F", "82 °F", "132 °F"], answer: 0, explain: "t(F) = (9/5)(50) + 32 = 90 + 32 = 122 °F.", level: 1 },
      { q: "For an isotropic solid, the coefficient of volume expansion γ and the coefficient of linear expansion α are related by:", options: ["γ = α", "γ = 2α", "γ = α³", "γ = 3α"], answer: 3, explain: "For small expansions, V ∝ L³, so ΔV/V = 3ΔL/L, which gives γ = 3α.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A steel rail 30 m long is laid at 10 °C. If α = 1.2 × 10⁻⁵ °C⁻¹, the increase in its length at 40 °C is:", options: ["1.08 mm", "3.6 mm", "10.8 mm", "21.6 mm"], answer: 2, explain: "ΔL = αLΔT = 1.2 × 10⁻⁵ × 30 × 30 = 1.08 × 10⁻² m = 10.8 mm.", level: 2 },
      { q: "200 g of water at 80 °C is mixed with 300 g of water at 20 °C in an insulated vessel. The final temperature is:", options: ["56 °C", "44 °C", "50 °C", "36 °C"], answer: 1, explain: "Heat lost = heat gained: 200(80 − T) = 300(T − 20) → 16 000 + 6 000 = 500T → T = 44 °C.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Steam at 100 °C causes a more severe burn than water at 100 °C.\nReason (R): Steam gives out a large amount of latent heat when it condenses on the skin.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Each kilogram of steam releases about 22.6 × 10⁵ J on condensing, over and above the heat released as the water cools. This extra heat makes the burn worse, so R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A car's cooling system holds 5 kg of water (s = 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹). The engine transfers 4.2 × 10⁵ J of heat to this water. The rise in temperature of the water is:", options: ["40 °C", "10 °C", "84 °C", "20 °C"], answer: 3, explain: "ΔT = Q/(ms) = 4.2 × 10⁵/(5 × 4200) = 4.2 × 10⁵/21 000 = 20 °C. Water's high specific heat lets it carry away a lot of heat for a small rise in temperature.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The heat required to melt 100 g of ice at 0 °C (L = 3.33 × 10⁵ J kg⁻¹) is:", options: ["3.33 × 10³ J", "3.33 × 10⁴ J", "3.33 × 10⁵ J", "4.19 × 10⁴ J"], answer: 1, explain: "Q = mL = 0.1 × 3.33 × 10⁵ = 3.33 × 10⁴ J. The temperature remains 0 °C during melting.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A steel rod of cross-section 1 cm² is clamped rigidly at both ends and heated through 50 °C. If Y = 2 × 10¹¹ Pa and α = 1.2 × 10⁻⁵ °C⁻¹, the force it exerts on each clamp is:", options: ["1.2 × 10⁸ N", "2.4 × 10⁴ N", "1.2 × 10⁴ N", "6.0 × 10³ N"], answer: 2, explain: "Thermal stress = YαΔT = 2 × 10¹¹ × 1.2 × 10⁻⁵ × 50 = 1.2 × 10⁸ Pa. Force = stress × A = 1.2 × 10⁸ × 10⁻⁴ m² = 1.2 × 10⁴ N.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Rahul drops 50 g of ice at 0 °C into 400 g of water at 30 °C in an insulated cup. Take s(water) = 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹ and L = 3.3 × 10⁵ J kg⁻¹. The final temperature, once all the ice has melted, is about:", options: ["15 °C", "18 °C", "27 °C", "10 °C"], answer: 1, explain: "Water cooling to 0 °C releases 0.4 × 4200 × 30 = 50 400 J. Melting the ice needs 0.05 × 3.3 × 10⁵ = 16 500 J, leaving 33 900 J to warm 0.45 kg of water from 0 °C: T = 33 900/(0.45 × 4200) ≈ 18 °C. (27 °C results if the latent heat is forgotten.)", level: 3 },
      { q: "A blackbody is heated from 27 °C to 327 °C. The power it radiates becomes:", options: ["16 times", "8 times", "4 times", "2 times"], answer: 0, explain: "Stefan–Boltzmann law needs kelvin: T₁ = 300 K and T₂ = 600 K. Power ∝ T⁴, so the ratio is (600/300)⁴ = 16.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Lakes freeze from the top downwards.\nReason (R): Water has its maximum density at 0 °C.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "A is true. R is false: the maximum density of water is at 4 °C. As the lake cools, water at 4 °C sinks and the colder, lighter water stays on top and freezes first.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define specific heat capacity of a substance and write its SI unit.", a: "The specific heat capacity is the heat required to raise the temperature of unit mass of the substance by one kelvin: s = ΔQ/(mΔT). SI unit: J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State Wien's displacement law.", a: "The wavelength λₘ at which a blackbody emits its maximum radiation is inversely proportional to its absolute temperature: λₘT = constant = 2.9 × 10⁻³ m K. Hotter bodies emit their peak at shorter wavelengths.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "At what temperature do the Celsius and Fahrenheit scales give the same reading? Show your working.", a: "Let the common reading be x. F = (9/5)C + 32 gives x = (9/5)x + 32.\nSo x − 1.8x = 32, −0.8x = 32, hence x = **−40**.\nThe two scales read the same at −40 °C = −40 °F.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain why the water at the bottom of a deep frozen lake stays at about 4 °C.", a: "Water has maximum density at 4 °C (it contracts on heating from 0 to 4 °C). As the surface cools, water at 4 °C sinks to the bottom. Water colder than 4 °C is less dense and stays on top, where it freezes.\nIce is a poor conductor, so the layer of ice protects the water below, which stays near 4 °C and aquatic life survives.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A 0.25 kg piece of metal, heated to 100 °C, is dropped into 0.5 kg of water at 20 °C kept in an insulated calorimeter of negligible heat capacity. The final temperature is 30 °C. Find the specific heat capacity of the metal. (s of water = 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹)", a: "Heat lost by metal = heat gained by water.\n0.25 × s × (100 − 30) = 0.5 × 4200 × (30 − 20)\n17.5 s = 21 000\ns = **1200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Calculate the heat required to convert 50 g of ice at −10 °C into steam at 100 °C. (s of ice = 2100 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹, s of water = 4186 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹, L(f) = 3.33 × 10⁵ J kg⁻¹, L(v) = 22.6 × 10⁵ J kg⁻¹)", a: "1. Ice −10 °C → 0 °C: 0.05 × 2100 × 10 = 1050 J.\n2. Melting at 0 °C: 0.05 × 3.33 × 10⁵ = 16 650 J.\n3. Water 0 °C → 100 °C: 0.05 × 4186 × 100 = 20 930 J.\n4. Boiling at 100 °C: 0.05 × 22.6 × 10⁵ = 113 000 J.\nTotal = 1050 + 16 650 + 20 930 + 113 000 = 151 630 J ≈ **1.52 × 10⁵ J**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A steel rod fixed rigidly between two supports is cooled from 40 °C to 20 °C. Find the thermal stress developed and say whether it is tensile or compressive. (Y = 2 × 10¹¹ Pa, α = 1.2 × 10⁻⁵ °C⁻¹)", a: "1. On cooling, the rod tries to contract by ΔL = αLΔT, but the supports prevent it.\n2. Thermal stress = YαΔT = 2 × 10¹¹ × 1.2 × 10⁻⁵ × 20 = **4.8 × 10⁷ Pa**.\n3. The supports hold the rod from contracting, so it is stretched: the stress is **tensile**. (The length of the rod does not matter.)", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A copper rod and a steel rod, each of length 0.5 m and cross-section 2 cm², are joined end to end. The free end of the copper rod is kept at 100 °C and the free end of the steel rod at 0 °C. Taking K(copper) = 400 W m⁻¹ K⁻¹ and K(steel) = 50 W m⁻¹ K⁻¹, find the temperature of the junction and the rate of heat flow in the steady state.", a: "1. In the steady state the same heat flows through both rods: K₁A(100 − T)/L = K₂A(T − 0)/L.\n2. 400(100 − T) = 50T → 40 000 = 450T → **T ≈ 88.9 °C**.\n3. Heat flow H = K₂A(T − 0)/L = 50 × 2 × 10⁻⁴ × 88.9/0.5\n4. = 0.8889/0.5 ≈ **1.78 W**.\n5. Check with copper: 400 × 2 × 10⁻⁴ × 11.1/0.5 ≈ 1.78 W. The steel rod has the larger temperature drop because it conducts poorly.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Describe the three modes of heat transfer. Give the formula for the rate of conduction through a slab and the Stefan–Boltzmann law, and one everyday example of each mode.", a: "1. **Conduction**: transfer of heat through matter without bulk movement of the matter, from hot to cold part. Rate H = KA(T₁ − T₂)/L. Example: the handle of a hot pan heating up.\n2. **Convection**: heat transfer by actual movement of a fluid, either natural or forced. Example: a sea breeze, or the circulation of water in a heated pot.\n3. **Radiation**: heat transfer by electromagnetic waves; it needs no medium and travels at the speed of light. Example: warmth from the Sun.\n4. Stefan–Boltzmann law: the power radiated is H = eσAT⁴ (e = 1 for a blackbody, T in kelvin).\n5. Good conductors have high K (metals); poor conductors (air, wood, glass wool) are used as insulators.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A 1 kW electric heater (all its heat goes into the substance) is used to heat 0.5 kg of ice at 0 °C. Take L(f) = 3.33 × 10⁵ J kg⁻¹ and s(water) = 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.\n(i) How long does it take to melt all the ice?\n(ii) How much longer does it then take to raise the water from 0 °C to 100 °C?\n(iii) Why does the temperature stay at 0 °C while the ice melts although heat is being supplied?", a: "(i) Heat needed = mL = 0.5 × 3.33 × 10⁵ = 1.665 × 10⁵ J. Time = Q/P = 1.665 × 10⁵/1000 = **166.5 s ≈ 167 s**.\n(ii) Q = ms ΔT = 0.5 × 4200 × 100 = 2.1 × 10⁵ J. Time = 2.1 × 10⁵/1000 = **210 s**.\n(iii) During melting, the supplied heat (latent heat) is used to break the bonds holding the molecules in the solid, increasing their potential energy rather than their kinetic energy, so the temperature does not rise until all the ice has melted.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "thermodynamics": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The zeroth law of thermodynamics leads to the concept of:", options: ["internal energy", "entropy", "temperature", "heat"], answer: 2, explain: "If A and B are each in thermal equilibrium with C, they are in equilibrium with each other. The property common to bodies in thermal equilibrium is defined as temperature.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In an isothermal process of an ideal gas, the change in internal energy is:", options: ["zero", "positive", "negative", "equal to the heat absorbed"], answer: 0, explain: "The internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on temperature. With T constant, ΔU = 0, so all the heat absorbed becomes work.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Mayer's relation for an ideal gas is:", options: ["Cₚ + Cᵥ = R", "Cₚ − Cᵥ = R", "Cₚ/Cᵥ = R", "Cₚ × Cᵥ = R"], answer: 1, explain: "Cₚ exceeds Cᵥ by the work done per mole per kelvin during expansion at constant pressure, which is R.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The work done by a gas in an isochoric (constant volume) process is:", options: ["PΔV", "μRΔT", "μCᵥΔT", "zero"], answer: 3, explain: "Work = PΔV, and ΔV = 0 at constant volume, so no work is done. All heat supplied raises the internal energy.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The efficiency of a heat engine that absorbs Q₁ from the source and rejects Q₂ to the sink is:", options: ["Q₂/Q₁", "1 − Q₂/Q₁", "W/Q₂", "Q₁/Q₂"], answer: 1, explain: "η = W/Q₁ = (Q₁ − Q₂)/Q₁ = 1 − Q₂/Q₁.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A gas absorbs 500 J of heat and does 200 J of work. The change in its internal energy is:", options: ["700 J", "300 J", "200 J", "500 J"], answer: 1, explain: "First law: ΔQ = ΔU + ΔW, so ΔU = 500 − 200 = 300 J.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Two moles of an ideal gas are heated at constant pressure from 300 K to 400 K. The work done by the gas is (R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹):", options: ["831 J", "3324 J", "1662 J", "166 J"], answer: 2, explain: "At constant pressure W = PΔV = μRΔT = 2 × 8.31 × 100 = 1662 J.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): An ideal gas cools when it expands adiabatically.\nReason (R): In an adiabatic process, the work done by the gas is at the cost of its internal energy.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "With ΔQ = 0, the first law gives ΔW = −ΔU. The gas does positive work, so its internal energy, and hence its temperature, falls.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The efficiency of a Carnot engine is independent of the working substance.\nReason (R): A Carnot cycle consists of two isothermal and two adiabatic processes.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 1, explain: "Both are true. But the independence follows from Carnot's theorem and the fact that η = 1 − T₂/T₁ for any reversible engine; the description of the cycle does not by itself explain it.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A small experimental engine takes 1200 J per cycle from a hot reservoir at 600 K and rejects heat to surroundings at 300 K. The maximum work it can deliver per cycle is:", options: ["900 J", "300 J", "1200 J", "600 J"], answer: 3, explain: "The maximum efficiency is that of a Carnot engine: η = 1 − 300/600 = 0.5. W = ηQ₁ = 0.5 × 1200 = 600 J.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A gas follows a clockwise rectangular cycle on a P–V diagram, with pressure between 1 × 10⁵ Pa and 3 × 10⁵ Pa and volume between 2 L and 5 L. In one cycle it absorbs 1500 J in total from hot sources. The heat rejected per cycle is:", options: ["600 J", "1500 J", "900 J", "2100 J"], answer: 2, explain: "Net work = area of the rectangle = (3 − 1) × 10⁵ × (5 − 2) × 10⁻³ = 600 J. For a cycle ΔU = 0, so heat absorbed − heat rejected = 600 J, giving 1500 − 600 = 900 J.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A monatomic ideal gas (γ = 5/3) at 300 K is compressed adiabatically to 1/8 of its initial volume. The final temperature is:", options: ["1200 K", "600 K", "2400 K", "900 K"], answer: 0, explain: "TV^(γ−1) = constant. T₂ = T₁(V₁/V₂)^(2/3) = 300 × 8^(2/3) = 300 × 4 = 1200 K.", level: 3 },
      { q: "One mole of an ideal gas at 300 K expands isothermally to twice its volume. The work done by the gas is (R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹, ln 2 = 0.693):", options: ["2.49 kJ", "1.2 kJ", "3.46 kJ", "1.73 kJ"], answer: 3, explain: "W = μRT ln(V₂/V₁) = 1 × 8.31 × 300 × 0.693 ≈ 1728 J ≈ 1.73 kJ.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The engine of a power plant works as a Carnot engine with a sink at 300 K and an efficiency of 40%. Engineers want to raise the efficiency to 50% by changing only the source temperature. By how much must the source temperature be raised?", options: ["100 K", "50 K", "150 K", "200 K"], answer: 0, explain: "η = 1 − T₂/T₁. For 40%: 300/T₁ = 0.6, so T₁ = 500 K. For 50%: 300/T₁ = 0.5, so T₁ = 600 K. The rise is 100 K.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "State the zeroth law of thermodynamics.", a: "If two systems A and B are each in thermal equilibrium with a third system C, then A and B are in thermal equilibrium with each other. This gives the idea of temperature.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State the Kelvin–Planck statement of the second law of thermodynamics.", a: "No process is possible whose sole result is the absorption of heat from a reservoir and the complete conversion of the heat into work. Hence no heat engine can have 100% efficiency.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between an isothermal and an adiabatic process, and write the equation connecting P and V for each.", a: "Isothermal: the temperature is constant (the system exchanges heat with surroundings, ΔU = 0 for an ideal gas); PV = constant.\nAdiabatic: no heat is exchanged (ΔQ = 0), so the temperature changes; PV^γ = constant, where γ = Cₚ/Cᵥ.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Two moles of a monatomic ideal gas are heated from 300 K to 350 K at constant volume. Find the heat absorbed. (R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹)", a: "At constant volume Q = μCᵥΔT, with Cᵥ = (3/2)R for a monatomic gas.\nQ = 2 × (3/2) × 8.31 × 50 = 3 × 8.31 × 50\n= **1246.5 J ≈ 1.25 × 10³ J**. (No work is done, so ΔU = Q.)", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Derive Mayer's relation Cₚ − Cᵥ = R for an ideal gas.", a: "1. At constant volume, the heat given to μ moles for a temperature rise dT is dQ = μCᵥdT = dU, as no work is done.\n2. At constant pressure, dQ = μCₚdT = dU + PdV. For an ideal gas dU depends only on T, so it is the same μCᵥdT.\n3. Hence μCₚdT = μCᵥdT + PdV. From PV = μRT at constant P, PdV = μRdT.\n4. Therefore Cₚ = Cᵥ + R, i.e. **Cₚ − Cᵥ = R**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A gas expands from 1 L to 3 L at a constant pressure of 2 × 10⁵ Pa while absorbing 800 J of heat. Find the work done by the gas and the change in its internal energy.", a: "ΔV = 3 − 1 = 2 L = 2 × 10⁻³ m³.\nWork done W = PΔV = 2 × 10⁵ × 2 × 10⁻³ = **400 J**.\nFirst law: ΔU = Q − W = 800 − 400 = **400 J** (the internal energy increases, so the temperature rises).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A Carnot engine works between a source at 627 °C and a sink at 27 °C. Find its efficiency. If it absorbs 3000 J of heat from the source per cycle, find the work done and the heat rejected per cycle.", a: "T₁ = 627 + 273 = 900 K, T₂ = 27 + 273 = 300 K.\nη = 1 − T₂/T₁ = 1 − 300/900 = 2/3 ≈ **66.7%**.\nWork W = ηQ₁ = (2/3) × 3000 = **2000 J**.\nHeat rejected Q₂ = Q₁ − W = **1000 J** (check: Q₂/Q₁ = T₂/T₁ = 1/3).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the four steps of a Carnot cycle and show that its efficiency is η = 1 − T₂/T₁.", a: "1. **Isothermal expansion** at T₁: the gas takes in Q₁ = μRT₁ ln(V₂/V₁).\n2. **Adiabatic expansion**: the gas cools from T₁ to T₂, doing work at the cost of its internal energy.\n3. **Isothermal compression** at T₂: the gas gives out Q₂ = μRT₂ ln(V₃/V₄) to the sink.\n4. **Adiabatic compression**: the gas returns to its initial state, with temperature rising from T₂ to T₁.\n5. For the two adiabatic steps, T₁V₂^(γ−1) = T₂V₃^(γ−1) and T₁V₁^(γ−1) = T₂V₄^(γ−1), so V₂/V₁ = V₃/V₄. Hence Q₁/Q₂ = T₁/T₂ and **η = 1 − Q₂/Q₁ = 1 − T₂/T₁**.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Derive the expression for the work done by μ moles of an ideal gas during an isothermal expansion from volume V₁ to V₂ at temperature T. Hence find the work done when 1 mol of gas at 300 K expands from 2 L to 8 L. (R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹, ln 4 = 1.386)", a: "1. For a small change, dW = P dV, with P = μRT/V from the gas equation.\n2. W = ∫ P dV from V₁ to V₂ = μRT ∫ dV/V.\n3. T is constant, so **W = μRT ln(V₂/V₁)**.\n4. Numerical: W = 1 × 8.31 × 300 × ln(8/2) = 2493 × 1.386\n5. ≈ 3456 J ≈ **3.46 × 10³ J**. This equals the heat absorbed (ΔU = 0 for an isothermal process).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: An ideal gas is taken round the cycle A → B → C → D → A on a P–V diagram. A (2 × 10⁵ Pa, 1 L) → B (2 × 10⁵ Pa, 3 L) → C (1 × 10⁵ Pa, 3 L) → D (1 × 10⁵ Pa, 1 L) → A.\n(i) Find the work done by the gas in A → B.\n(ii) Find the work done in C → D, and say whether it is done by or on the gas.\n(iii) Find the net work done in one cycle.", a: "(i) A → B is isobaric: W = PΔV = 2 × 10⁵ × (3 − 1) × 10⁻³ = **+400 J** (done by the gas).\n(ii) C → D is isobaric compression: W = 1 × 10⁵ × (1 − 3) × 10⁻³ = **−200 J**, i.e. 200 J of work is done on the gas.\n(iii) B → C and D → A are at constant volume (no work). Net work = 400 − 200 = **200 J**, equal to the area of the rectangle (1 × 10⁵ Pa × 2 × 10⁻³ m³). The cycle is clockwise, so the gas does net positive work.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "kinetic-theory": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The average translational kinetic energy of a molecule of an ideal gas at absolute temperature T is:", options: ["kBT", "½ kBT", "(3/2) kBT", "3 kBT"], answer: 2, explain: "Each of the three translational degrees of freedom has energy ½kBT, so the average translational KE is (3/2)kBT.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The number of degrees of freedom of a rigid diatomic molecule is:", options: ["5", "3", "6", "7"], answer: 0, explain: "A rigid diatomic molecule has 3 translational and 2 rotational degrees of freedom (rotation about its own axis is not counted), a total of 5.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The ratio γ = Cₚ/Cᵥ for a monatomic ideal gas is:", options: ["7/5", "4/3", "9/7", "5/3"], answer: 3, explain: "Cᵥ = (3/2)R and Cₚ = (5/2)R, so γ = 5/3.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The value of the Boltzmann constant is:", options: ["8.31 J K⁻¹", "1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹", "6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹", "1.38 × 10⁻²³ J"], answer: 1, explain: "kB = R/N(A) = 8.31/6.02 × 10²³ = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The average translational kinetic energy of the molecules of an ideal gas depends only on:", options: ["the nature of the gas", "the mass of a molecule", "the absolute temperature", "the volume of the container"], answer: 2, explain: "Average KE = (3/2)kBT. It is the same for all ideal gases at the same temperature.", level: 1 },

      { q: "The root mean square speed of oxygen molecules (M = 32 g mol⁻¹) at 300 K is about (R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹):", options: ["342 m s⁻¹", "684 m s⁻¹", "483 m s⁻¹", "241 m s⁻¹"], answer: 2, explain: "v(rms) = √(3RT/M) = √(3 × 8.31 × 300/0.032) = √(2.34 × 10⁵) ≈ 483 m s⁻¹. (Using M in grams instead of kg gives a wrong answer by a factor of √1000.)", level: 2 },
      { q: "The ratio of the rms speed of the molecules of a gas at 27 °C to that at 327 °C is:", options: ["1 : 2", "1 : √2", "√2 : 1", "2 : 1"], answer: 1, explain: "v(rms) ∝ √T with T in kelvin: 300 K and 600 K. The ratio is √300 : √600 = 1 : √2.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The average translational kinetic energies of helium and neon molecules at the same temperature are equal.\nReason (R): The average translational kinetic energy of a molecule depends only on the absolute temperature.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "KE = (3/2)kBT is independent of the nature of the gas, so equal temperature means equal average translational KE (though neon atoms move more slowly than helium atoms).", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): At a given temperature, all the molecules of a gas move with the same speed.\nReason (R): Collisions between molecules continually change their speeds, so the molecules have a wide distribution of speeds.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "Random collisions keep changing the speed of each molecule, so there is a spread of speeds around the rms value. A is false; R is true.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A gas cylinder of volume 10 L holds 2 mol of an ideal gas at 300 K. The pressure of the gas is about (R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹):", options: ["5.0 × 10⁴ Pa", "5.0 × 10⁵ Pa", "2.5 × 10⁵ Pa", "1.0 × 10⁶ Pa"], answer: 1, explain: "P = μRT/V = 2 × 8.31 × 300/(10 × 10⁻³ m³) = 4986/0.01 ≈ 5.0 × 10⁵ Pa.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A gas mixture contains equal numbers of moles of helium (monatomic) and hydrogen (rigid diatomic). The ratio Cₚ/Cᵥ of the mixture is:", options: ["1.67", "1.40", "1.50", "1.53"], answer: 2, explain: "Cᵥ(mix) = (3R/2 + 5R/2)/2 = 2R; Cₚ(mix) = Cᵥ + R = 3R. So γ = 3/2 = 1.50. (1.53 is the wrong average of the two γ values.)", level: 3 },
      { q: "At 300 K the rms speed of oxygen molecules is v. At what temperature will the rms speed of hydrogen (H₂) molecules be the same?", options: ["4800 K", "75 K", "300 K", "18.75 K"], answer: 3, explain: "v ∝ √(T/M). For equal v, T_H/M_H = T_O/M_O, so T_H = 300 × (2/32) = 18.75 K.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Two moles of a rigid diatomic gas are heated at constant pressure so that the temperature rises by 20 K. The heat supplied is (R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹):", options: ["831 J", "1163 J", "1662 J", "2326 J"], answer: 1, explain: "For a rigid diatomic gas Cₚ = (7/2)R. Q = μCₚΔT = 2 × 3.5 × 8.31 × 20 = 1163 J. (831 J would be the heat at constant volume.)", level: 3 },
      { q: "A supplier fills a rigid cylinder with 4 g of hydrogen (H₂) and 4 g of helium (He). What fraction of the total pressure is due to helium? (Molar masses: H₂ = 2 g, He = 4 g)", options: ["1/3", "1/2", "2/3", "1/4"], answer: 0, explain: "Moles of H₂ = 4/2 = 2 and moles of He = 4/4 = 1. Partial pressure is proportional to the number of moles (Dalton's law), so the fraction for helium = 1/(1 + 2) = 1/3.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is meant by degrees of freedom of a molecule?", a: "The degrees of freedom of a molecule are the number of independent ways in which it can possess energy (independent coordinates needed to specify its motion). For example, a monatomic molecule has 3 (translational) and a rigid diatomic molecule has 5.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State Dalton's law of partial pressures.", a: "The total pressure exerted by a mixture of non-reacting ideal gases is the sum of the partial pressures that each gas would exert if it alone occupied the whole container at the same temperature.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State two assumptions of the kinetic theory of gases.", a: "Any two of: (1) a gas consists of a very large number of identical molecules in continuous random motion; (2) the size of molecules is negligible compared with the distance between them; (3) there are no forces between molecules except during collisions; (4) collisions are perfectly elastic and of negligible duration.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Calculate the ratio of the rms speed of hydrogen molecules to that of oxygen molecules at the same temperature.", a: "v(rms) = √(3RT/M), so at the same T, v ∝ 1/√M.\nv(H₂) : v(O₂) = √(M(O₂)/M(H₂)) = √(32/2) = √16 = **4 : 1**.\nHydrogen molecules move four times as fast.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Using the law of equipartition of energy, find Cᵥ, Cₚ and γ for (a) a monatomic gas and (b) a rigid diatomic gas.", a: "Each degree of freedom contributes ½RT per mole to the internal energy, and Cₚ = Cᵥ + R.\n(a) Monatomic: f = 3, U = (3/2)RT, so Cᵥ = (3/2)R, Cₚ = (5/2)R, γ = **5/3**.\n(b) Rigid diatomic: f = 5, U = (5/2)RT, so Cᵥ = (5/2)R, Cₚ = (7/2)R, γ = **7/5**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Starting from P = ⅓ n m⟨v²⟩ and the ideal gas equation, show that the average translational kinetic energy of a molecule is (3/2)kBT.", a: "1. For N molecules in volume V, n = N/V, so PV = ⅓ N m⟨v²⟩.\n2. The ideal gas equation in molecular form is PV = N kB T.\n3. Equating, ⅓ N m⟨v²⟩ = N kB T, which gives ½ m⟨v²⟩ = **(3/2) kB T**. It depends only on T, and the rms speed is √(3kBT/m).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Find the number of molecules in 2.0 L of a gas at 0 °C and 1 atm. (One mole occupies 22.4 L at STP; N(A) = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)", a: "Number of moles n = 2.0/22.4 = 0.0893 mol.\nN = n × N(A) = 0.0893 × 6.02 × 10²³\n≈ **5.4 × 10²² molecules**.\n(Check: N = PV/kBT = 1.013 × 10⁵ × 2 × 10⁻³/(1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 273) ≈ 5.4 × 10²².)", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Derive an expression for the pressure exerted by an ideal gas on the walls of its container, using the kinetic theory.", a: "1. Take a cube of side l with N molecules of mass m. A molecule with velocity components (vₓ, vᵧ, v_z) strikes the wall perpendicular to x.\n2. The collision is elastic, so its momentum change is 2mvₓ.\n3. It returns to the same wall after time 2l/vₓ, so the force it exerts = 2mvₓ/(2l/vₓ) = mvₓ²/l.\n4. Total force by all molecules F = (m/l)Σvₓ² = (m/l)N⟨vₓ²⟩. Pressure P = F/l² = mN⟨vₓ²⟩/V.\n5. For random motion ⟨vₓ²⟩ = ⟨v²⟩/3. So **P = ⅓ (N/V) m⟨v²⟩ = ⅓ n m⟨v²⟩**, or P = ⅓ρ⟨v²⟩.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "A vessel contains 2 mol of helium and 3 mol of rigid diatomic hydrogen at 300 K. Find (a) the total internal energy, (b) the molar specific heat Cᵥ of the mixture, (c) γ of the mixture. (R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹)", a: "1. U(He) = 2 × (3/2)RT = 3RT; U(H₂) = 3 × (5/2)RT = 7.5RT.\n2. (a) Total U = 10.5RT = 10.5 × 8.31 × 300 = 26 176 J ≈ **2.62 × 10⁴ J**.\n3. (b) Cᵥ(mix) = (2 × 1.5R + 3 × 2.5R)/5 = 10.5R/5 = **2.1R** ≈ 17.4 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.\n4. Cₚ(mix) = Cᵥ + R = 3.1R.\n5. (c) γ = 3.1R/2.1R = **1.48**.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Air in a classroom is mostly nitrogen (molar mass 28 g mol⁻¹) at a temperature of 27 °C. Take R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ and kB = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.\n(i) Calculate the rms speed of nitrogen molecules.\n(ii) Calculate the average translational kinetic energy of one molecule.\n(iii) By what factor does the rms speed change if the air is heated to 927 °C? Give the new value.", a: "(i) T = 300 K, M = 0.028 kg mol⁻¹. v(rms) = √(3RT/M) = √(3 × 8.31 × 300/0.028) = √(2.67 × 10⁵) ≈ **517 m s⁻¹**.\n(ii) KE = (3/2)kBT = 1.5 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 300 = **6.21 × 10⁻²¹ J**.\n(iii) 927 °C = 1200 K, which is 4 times 300 K. Since v ∝ √T, the speed doubles to about 2 × 517 ≈ **1.03 × 10³ m s⁻¹**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "oscillations": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The SI unit of angular frequency ω is:", options: ["Hz", "rad s⁻¹", "s", "rad"], answer: 1, explain: "ω = 2π/T = 2πν is the angle covered per unit time, so its unit is radian per second.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In simple harmonic motion, the speed of the particle is maximum:", options: ["at the extreme positions", "halfway between the mean and extreme positions", "at the mean position", "at all positions equally"], answer: 2, explain: "v = ω√(A² − x²) is largest when x = 0, i.e. at the mean position, where v = ωA.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The time period of a mass m oscillating on a spring of force constant k is:", options: ["2π√(k/m)", "(1/2π)√(m/k)", "2π√(mk)", "2π√(m/k)"], answer: 3, explain: "From F = −kx, ω = √(k/m), so T = 2π/ω = 2π√(m/k).", level: 1 },
      { q: "In SHM, the phase difference between acceleration and displacement is:", options: ["π", "π/2", "0", "3π/2"], answer: 0, explain: "a = −ω²x: acceleration is always opposite to displacement, so they are π out of phase.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following motions is periodic but not oscillatory?", options: ["Motion of a pendulum bob", "Uniform circular motion", "A mass moving on a spring", "A vibrating guitar string"], answer: 1, explain: "Uniform circular motion repeats at regular intervals but is not a to-and-fro motion about a mean position, so it is not oscillatory.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A block of mass 0.5 kg is attached to a spring of force constant 200 N m⁻¹. Its time period of oscillation is:", options: ["3.14 s", "0.628 s", "0.314 s", "0.157 s"], answer: 2, explain: "T = 2π√(m/k) = 2π√(0.5/200) = 2π × 0.05 = 0.314 s.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A simple pendulum of length 0.4 m oscillates on Earth with small amplitude. Taking g = 10 m s⁻², its time period is about:", options: ["0.63 s", "1.26 s", "2.51 s", "0.40 s"], answer: 1, explain: "T = 2π√(L/g) = 2π√(0.4/10) = 2π × 0.2 = 1.26 s.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A particle executes SHM given by x = 0.05 sin(20t), with x in metres and t in seconds. Its maximum speed is:", options: ["0.05 m s⁻¹", "20 m s⁻¹", "0.25 m s⁻¹", "1 m s⁻¹"], answer: 3, explain: "A = 0.05 m and ω = 20 rad s⁻¹, so v(max) = ωA = 20 × 0.05 = 1 m s⁻¹.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): At the extreme position of SHM the speed of the particle is zero but its acceleration is maximum.\nReason (R): The acceleration in SHM is given by a = −ω²x, which is largest in magnitude when |x| = A.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "At |x| = A the velocity v = ω√(A² − x²) = 0, and the acceleration magnitude ω²|x| = ω²A is maximum, as R states.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A car of mass 1000 kg rests on four identical springs, each of force constant 10⁴ N m⁻¹, which act in parallel. The time period of vertical oscillations of the car body is about:", options: ["1.99 s", "0.50 s", "0.99 s", "3.14 s"], answer: 2, explain: "Springs in parallel: k = 4 × 10⁴ N m⁻¹. T = 2π√(m/k) = 2π√(1000/40 000) = 2π × 0.158 ≈ 0.99 s. Using a single spring would wrongly give 1.99 s.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A particle in SHM has amplitude 10 cm. At a displacement of 6 cm from the mean position, the ratio of its kinetic energy to its potential energy is:", options: ["9 : 16", "3 : 4", "4 : 3", "16 : 9"], answer: 3, explain: "KE = ½k(A² − x²) = ½k(100 − 36) and PE = ½kx² = ½k(36), in units of cm². So KE : PE = 64 : 36 = 16 : 9.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A mass m attached to a spring oscillates with time period T. The spring is cut into 4 equal parts and the same mass is attached to one part. The new time period is:", options: ["T/4", "T/2", "2T", "4T"], answer: 1, explain: "A spring cut into 4 equal parts has 4 times the force constant (k′ = 4k), because each part is stiffer. T′ = 2π√(m/4k) = T/2.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A student hangs a mass of 0.4 kg from a spring and finds that it stretches the spring by 4 cm in equilibrium (g = 10 m s⁻²). She then pulls it down slightly and releases it. The time period of the oscillations is about:", options: ["0.40 s", "0.63 s", "0.25 s", "1.26 s"], answer: 0, explain: "k = mg/x = 0.4 × 10/0.04 = 100 N m⁻¹. T = 2π√(m/k) = 2π√(0.4/100) = 2π × 0.0632 ≈ 0.40 s. (Equivalently T = 2π√(x/g).)", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The time period of a spring–mass system is the same on the Moon as on Earth.\nReason (R): The time period of a spring–mass system depends on the acceleration due to gravity.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "T = 2π√(m/k) involves only m and k, not g, so A is true (the equilibrium position shifts but the period does not change). R is false.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is the phase difference between the velocity and the displacement of a particle in SHM?", a: "Velocity leads displacement by π/2 (90°). For x = A cos(ωt + φ), v = −ωA sin(ωt + φ) = ωA cos(ωt + φ + π/2).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Is uniform circular motion simple harmonic? Explain.", a: "No. Uniform circular motion is periodic, but it is not an oscillation about a mean position. However, the projection of a particle in uniform circular motion on any diameter of the circle executes SHM.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Show that the motion described by x = A cos ωt satisfies the condition for SHM, a = −ω²x.", a: "v = dx/dt = −ωA sin ωt.\na = dv/dt = −ω²A cos ωt.\nSince x = A cos ωt, a = **−ω²x**: the acceleration is proportional to the displacement and directed towards the mean position, which is the condition for SHM.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A particle moves with acceleration a = −36x (SI units), where x is the displacement from the mean position. If its amplitude is 5 cm, find the time period and the maximum speed.", a: "Comparing with a = −ω²x, ω² = 36, so ω = 6 rad s⁻¹.\nT = 2π/ω = 2π/6 = π/3 ≈ **1.05 s**.\nv(max) = ωA = 6 × 0.05 = **0.30 m s⁻¹**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "The displacement of a particle in SHM is x = 0.2 cos(4t) metres. Find the amplitude, time period, maximum speed and the speed when x = 0.1 m.", a: "Amplitude A = **0.2 m**, ω = 4 rad s⁻¹.\nT = 2π/4 ≈ **1.57 s**.\nv(max) = ωA = 4 × 0.2 = **0.8 m s⁻¹**.\nAt x = 0.1 m: v = ω√(A² − x²) = 4√(0.04 − 0.01) = 4 × 0.1732 ≈ **0.69 m s⁻¹**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Show that the total energy of a particle in SHM is ½kA², and find the displacement at which its kinetic energy equals its potential energy.", a: "1. PE at displacement x: U = ½kx². KE: K = ½mv² = ½mω²(A² − x²) = ½k(A² − x²), as k = mω².\n2. Total E = K + U = ½k(A² − x²) + ½kx² = **½kA²**, constant.\n3. For K = U: ½k(A² − x²) = ½kx² → x² = A²/2, so **x = A/√2** ≈ 0.71 A.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Derive the expression for the time period of a simple pendulum for small oscillations.", a: "1. Let a bob of mass m on a string of length L be displaced by a small angle θ; the arc displacement is x = Lθ.\n2. The restoring force along the arc is −mg sinθ ≈ −mgθ (sinθ ≈ θ for small θ).\n3. So F = −mg(x/L) = −(mg/L)x. This is the SHM form F = −kx with k = mg/L.\n4. ω = √(k/m) = √(g/L), hence **T = 2π√(L/g)**, independent of the mass of the bob.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Obtain expressions for the kinetic, potential and total energy of a particle executing SHM of amplitude A and angular frequency ω. State where each is maximum and give the frequency with which KE varies.", a: "1. Displacement x = A cos ωt, velocity v = −ωA sin ωt.\n2. KE = ½mv² = ½mω²A² sin²ωt = ½k(A² − x²).\n3. PE = ½kx² = ½mω²A² cos²ωt.\n4. Total E = ½mω²A² = ½kA², constant and ∝ A².\n5. KE is maximum (equal to E) at the mean position and zero at the extremes; PE is maximum (E) at the extremes and zero at the mean position. Both vary with period T/2, i.e. twice the frequency of the oscillation.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "A block of mass 0.5 kg attached to a spring of force constant 50 N m⁻¹ slides on a frictionless horizontal table. It is pulled 8 cm from the mean position and released. Find (a) the angular frequency and time period, (b) the total energy, (c) the maximum speed, (d) the kinetic and potential energy at x = 4 cm.", a: "(a) ω = √(k/m) = √(50/0.5) = **10 rad s⁻¹**; T = 2π/10 ≈ **0.63 s**.\n(b) E = ½kA² = ½ × 50 × (0.08)² = 25 × 0.0064 = **0.16 J**.\n(c) v(max) = ωA = 10 × 0.08 = **0.8 m s⁻¹** (check: ½mv² = ½ × 0.5 × 0.64 = 0.16 J).\n(d) PE at x = 0.04 m: ½kx² = 25 × 0.0016 = **0.04 J**; KE = E − PE = 0.16 − 0.04 = **0.12 J**.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A simple pendulum of length 1.0 m is used as a timekeeper in a school laboratory on Earth, where g = 9.8 m s⁻². Meera wonders how it would behave on the Moon, where g = 1.6 m s⁻².\n(i) Find the time period on Earth.\n(ii) Find the time period of the same pendulum on the Moon.\n(iii) What length would give a period of 2.0 s on the Moon?", a: "(i) T = 2π√(L/g) = 2π√(1.0/9.8) = 2π × 0.3194 ≈ **2.0 s**.\n(ii) T = 2π√(1.0/1.6) = 2π × 0.7906 ≈ **5.0 s**: the pendulum swings more slowly because g is smaller.\n(iii) L = gT²/(4π²) = 1.6 × 4/39.48 ≈ 0.162 m, i.e. about **16 cm**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "waves": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "A progressive wave transports:", options: ["matter from one place to another", "energy, without transporting matter", "electric charge", "the particles of the medium along with it"], answer: 1, explain: "The particles of the medium only oscillate about their mean positions; the wave carries the disturbance and energy forward.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following cannot travel through a vacuum?", options: ["Light", "X-rays", "Sound", "Radio waves"], answer: 2, explain: "Sound is a mechanical wave and needs a material medium. Light, X-rays and radio waves are electromagnetic waves.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In a longitudinal wave the particles of the medium vibrate:", options: ["perpendicular to the direction of propagation", "in circles about the source", "only at the source", "along the direction of propagation"], answer: 3, explain: "A longitudinal wave (e.g. sound in air) is a series of compressions and rarefactions in which the particles move back and forth along the direction of travel.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The speed of a wave of frequency ν and wavelength λ is:", options: ["νλ", "ν/λ", "λ/ν", "ν²λ"], answer: 0, explain: "In one period T the wave advances one wavelength, so v = λ/T = νλ.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Two sources of sound of frequencies ν₁ and ν₂ (close to each other) produce beats. The beat frequency is:", options: ["ν₁ + ν₂", "|ν₁ − ν₂|", "ν₁ν₂", "(ν₁ + ν₂)/2"], answer: 1, explain: "The loudness rises and falls |ν₁ − ν₂| times per second.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A wave on a string is described by y = 0.01 sin(100t − 2x), where x and y are in metres and t is in seconds. The speed of the wave is:", options: ["25 m s⁻¹", "200 m s⁻¹", "50 m s⁻¹", "0.02 m s⁻¹"], answer: 2, explain: "Comparing with y = a sin(ωt − kx): ω = 100 rad s⁻¹, k = 2 rad m⁻¹. v = ω/k = 50 m s⁻¹.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A string of mass per unit length 0.01 kg m⁻¹ is stretched with a tension of 100 N. The speed of a transverse wave on it is:", options: ["10 m s⁻¹", "1000 m s⁻¹", "1 m s⁻¹", "100 m s⁻¹"], answer: 3, explain: "v = √(T/μ) = √(100/0.01) = √10⁴ = 100 m s⁻¹.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The speed of sound in air is 340 m s⁻¹. The fundamental frequency of an air column in a pipe closed at one end and 0.5 m long is:", options: ["340 Hz", "170 Hz", "85 Hz", "680 Hz"], answer: 1, explain: "For a closed pipe ν₁ = v/4L = 340/(4 × 0.5) = 170 Hz. (340 Hz would be the fundamental of an open pipe of the same length.)", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A string fixed at both ends can vibrate only at certain discrete frequencies.\nReason (R): The speed of a transverse wave on a string is given by v = √(T/μ).",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 1, explain: "Both statements are true, but the discrete frequencies arise from the boundary condition (nodes at both ends, so L = nλ/2), not from the formula for the wave speed.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A guitar string 0.65 m long, fixed at both ends, sounds a fundamental note of 330 Hz. The speed of the transverse waves on the string is:", options: ["215 m s⁻¹", "858 m s⁻¹", "330 m s⁻¹", "429 m s⁻¹"], answer: 3, explain: "For the fundamental, λ = 2L = 1.3 m, so v = νλ = 330 × 1.3 = 429 m s⁻¹.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A tuning fork of unknown frequency produces 4 beats per second with a standard fork of 256 Hz. When the unknown fork is loaded with a little wax, the number of beats per second decreases. The original frequency of the unknown fork was:", options: ["252 Hz", "256 Hz", "260 Hz", "264 Hz"], answer: 2, explain: "The frequency is 252 Hz or 260 Hz. Loading with wax lowers the frequency. If it were 252 Hz, lowering it would increase the beats; the beats decrease only if the frequency was 260 Hz.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A pipe closed at one end resonates at two successive frequencies of 300 Hz and 500 Hz. If the speed of sound is 340 m s⁻¹, the length of the pipe is:", options: ["0.57 m", "1.70 m", "0.425 m", "0.85 m"], answer: 3, explain: "A closed pipe has only odd harmonics, which differ by 2ν₁. So 2ν₁ = 200 Hz and ν₁ = 100 Hz (the resonances are the 3rd and 5th harmonics). L = v/4ν₁ = 340/400 = 0.85 m.", level: 3 },
      { q: "On a day when the temperature is 27 °C, a student claps near a cliff and hears the echo after 2.0 s. The speed of sound in air at 0 °C is 331 m s⁻¹. The distance to the cliff is about (take 0 °C = 273 K):", options: ["347 m", "331 m", "694 m", "174 m"], answer: 0, explain: "v ∝ √T, so v = 331 × √(300/273) ≈ 331 × 1.048 ≈ 347 m s⁻¹. The sound goes to the cliff and back in 2.0 s, so distance = vt/2 = 347 × 2.0/2 ≈ 347 m.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The speed of sound in air at constant temperature increases when the pressure is increased.\nReason (R): The speed of sound in a gas is given by v = √(γP/ρ).",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "R is correct, but at constant temperature P/ρ stays constant (density rises in proportion to pressure), so the speed does not change. A is false.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is Laplace's correction to Newton's formula for the speed of sound in a gas?", a: "Newton assumed the compressions and rarefactions in a sound wave are isothermal, giving v = √(P/ρ). Laplace showed they are adiabatic (too fast for heat exchange), so v = √(γP/ρ), which agrees with the measured value.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State the principle of superposition of waves.", a: "When two or more waves overlap in a medium, the resultant displacement of a particle at any instant is the algebraic sum of the displacements that each wave would produce on its own.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "A travelling wave is given by y = 0.02 sin(2πx − 100πt) (SI units). Find its frequency and speed, and the direction of propagation.", a: "Compare with y = a sin(kx − ωt): k = 2π, so λ = 2π/k = 1 m; ω = 100π, so ν = ω/2π = **50 Hz**.\nSpeed v = νλ = 50 × 1 = **50 m s⁻¹** (also ω/k = 100π/2π = 50).\nThe form (kx − ωt) means the wave travels in the **+x direction**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A wire of linear density 0.02 kg m⁻¹ is under a tension of 50 N. Find the speed of a transverse wave on it, and the new speed if the tension is increased to 200 N.", a: "v = √(T/μ) = √(50/0.02) = √2500 = **50 m s⁻¹**.\nFor T = 200 N: v = √(200/0.02) = √10 000 = **100 m s⁻¹**.\nQuadrupling the tension doubles the speed.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Calculate the speed of sound in air at STP using (a) Newton's formula and (b) Laplace's formula. Take P = 1.013 × 10⁵ Pa, density of air ρ = 1.29 kg m⁻³ and γ = 1.4. Why is the second result closer to the measured value?", a: "(a) v = √(P/ρ) = √(1.013 × 10⁵/1.29) = √78 527 ≈ **280 m s⁻¹**.\n(b) v = √(γP/ρ) = √(1.4 × 78 527) = √109 938 ≈ **331.6 m s⁻¹**.\nThe measured value (about 331 m s⁻¹) matches (b) because the pressure changes in a sound wave are so rapid that heat has no time to flow between compressions and rarefactions: the changes are adiabatic, not isothermal.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A string of length 0.6 m fixed at both ends carries waves of speed 120 m s⁻¹. Find the wavelength and frequency of the fundamental mode, and the frequencies of the second and third harmonics.", a: "For the fundamental, L = λ/2, so λ = 2L = **1.2 m**.\nν₁ = v/λ = 120/1.2 = **100 Hz** (= v/2L).\nFor a string fixed at both ends ν = nv/2L, so ν₂ = **200 Hz** and ν₃ = **300 Hz**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A pipe closed at one end is 0.5 m long. Find its first three resonant frequencies. How do they compare with those of an open pipe of the same length? (Speed of sound = 340 m s⁻¹)", a: "Closed pipe: ν = nv/4L with n = 1, 3, 5 (only odd harmonics).\nν₁ = 340/(4 × 0.5) = **170 Hz**; ν₃ = 3 × 170 = **510 Hz**; ν₅ = 5 × 170 = **850 Hz**.\nOpen pipe of the same length: ν = nv/2L with n = 1, 2, 3, giving **340 Hz, 680 Hz, 1020 Hz**, i.e. the fundamental is twice as high and all harmonics are present.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Two identical progressive waves travel in opposite directions along a string. Show that they form a standing wave, and find the positions of nodes and antinodes.", a: "1. Let y₁ = a sin(kx − ωt) (moving to +x) and y₂ = a sin(kx + ωt) (moving to −x).\n2. By superposition, y = y₁ + y₂ = 2a sin kx cos ωt.\n3. The amplitude 2a sin kx depends on position and the time factor cos ωt is common to all points, so the pattern does not travel: a standing wave.\n4. **Nodes** (zero amplitude): sin kx = 0 → x = nλ/2, n = 0, 1, 2 … (adjacent nodes are λ/2 apart).\n5. **Antinodes** (maximum amplitude 2a): sin kx = ±1 → x = (n + ½)λ/2, midway between nodes (λ/4 from a node).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "A string of length 0.8 m and mass 8 g is fixed at both ends and kept under a tension of 100 N. Find (a) the speed of waves on the string, (b) its fundamental frequency, (c) the number of beats per second heard when the fundamental is sounded with a 60 Hz tuning fork, (d) the fundamental frequency if the tension is made four times, (e) the wavelength of the third harmonic.", a: "μ = 0.008/0.8 = 0.01 kg m⁻¹.\n(a) v = √(T/μ) = √(100/0.01) = **100 m s⁻¹**.\n(b) ν₁ = v/2L = 100/1.6 = **62.5 Hz**.\n(c) Beats = |62.5 − 60| = **2.5 per second**.\n(d) ν ∝ √T, so quadrupling T doubles the frequency: **125 Hz**.\n(e) λ₃ = 2L/3 = 1.6/3 ≈ **0.53 m**.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: During a thunderstorm Meera sees a flash of lightning and hears the thunder 6.0 s later. Take the speed of sound in air as 340 m s⁻¹ and the speed of light as 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.\n(i) How far away did the lightning strike?\n(ii) Why can the time taken by the light be ignored?\n(iii) The speed of sound is proportional to √T. By what factor does it change when the air temperature rises from 27 °C to 327 °C?", a: "(i) Distance = speed × time = 340 × 6.0 = **2040 m** (about 2 km).\n(ii) Light takes 2040/(3 × 10⁸) ≈ 7 × 10⁻⁶ s, which is negligible compared with 6 s, so the delay is due to the sound alone.\n(iii) T rises from 300 K to 600 K, so v changes by √(600/300) = √2 ≈ **1.41 times**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
});
